{:edb/db-before-t 1858 :edb/db-after-t 1859 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535171 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:38:03.896Z" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124170 1100 "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2$. Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$." 13194139535171 false] [17592186124170 1100 "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124170 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(3,-1, 1) + ∇f(3,-1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3, -1, 1)= -2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}f_{x} &= yz & ⇒ & f_{x}(3,-1, 1) =-1, \\\\ f_{y}|= xz + 2y & ⇒ & f_{y}(3,-1, 1) = 1 \\\\ f_{z}|= xy & ⇒ & f_{z}(3,-1, 1) =-3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(3,-1) &= ⟨ - 1, 1,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(3,-1, 1) + ∇f(3,-1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩; =-2 + ⟨ - 1, 1,-3⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩; =-2 - (x - 3) + (y + 1) - 3(z - 1); =-2 - x + 3 + y + 1 - 3z + 3; = 5 - x + y - 3z\n$$" 13194139535171 false] [17592186124170 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124338 1100 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124338 1100 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$, estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9)$." 13194139535171 false] [17592186124338 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(1, 1) + ∇f(1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩; = f(1, 1) + ⟨f_{x}(1, 1), f_{y}(1, 1)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩; = 1 + ⟨f_{x}(1, 1), f_{y}(1, 1)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(1, 1) &= 2(1)(1) = 2 \\\\\nf_{y}(1, 1) &= (1)^{2} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9$, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(1.1, 0.9) \\mid \\approx 1 + ⟨2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨1.1 - 1, 0.9 - 1⟩; = 1 + ⟨2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩; = 1 + 0.2 - 0.1; = 1.1\n$$" 13194139535171 false] [17592186124338 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124340 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124340 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(1,2,5)=2, \\quad f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2$.\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$." 13194139535171 false] [17592186124340 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(1, 2, 5) + ∇f(1, 2, 5) \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩; = f(1, 2, 5) + ⟨f_{x}(1, 2, 5), f_{y}(1, 2, 5), f_{z}(1, 2, 5)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩; = 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01$, $y=2.02$, and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(1.01, 2.02, 4.95) \\mid \\approx 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨1.01 - 1, 2.02 - 2, 4.95 - 5⟩; = 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨0.01, 0.02,-0.05⟩; = 2 + 0.04 + 0.02 + 0.1; = 2.16\n$$" 13194139535171 false] [17592186124340 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335332 13194139535171 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335339 13194139535171 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335374 13194139535171 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335381 13194139535171 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335388 13194139535171 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335304 13194139535171 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335311 13194139535171 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335346 13194139535171 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335353 13194139535171 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335318 13194139535171 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335325 13194139535171 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335360 13194139535171 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335367 13194139535171 true] [17592186125170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535171 true] [17592186125170 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose that $f(x, y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(2, 5) = 6, f_{x}(2, 5) = 1, f_{y}(2, 5) =-1$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2, 5)$, estimate the value of $f(2.06, 4.92)$" 13194139535171 false] [17592186125170 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,5)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(2,5)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(2,5)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,5),$ estimate the value of $f(2.06,4.92)$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186125170 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,5)+∇f(2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,5),{f}_{y}(2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-5⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.06,4.92)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.06$ and $y=4.92,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.06,4.92) & ≈6+⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2.06-2,4.92-5⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.06,-0.08⟩ \\\\  & =6+(0.06)+(-1)(-0.08) \\\\  & =6+0.06+0.08 \\\\  & =6.14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186125173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535171 true] [17592186125173 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f(x, y, z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(3, 3, 1) = 3, f_{x}(x, y, z) = \\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}, f_{y}(x, y, z) = \\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}, f_{z}(x, y, z) = \\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, 3, 1)$, estimate the value of $f(3.01, 3.02, 0.98)$." 13194139535171 false] [17592186125173 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,3,1)=3,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}},\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,3,1),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,3.02,0.98).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186125173 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,3,1)+∇f(3,3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-3,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈3+⟨{f}_{x}(3,3,1),{f}_{y}(3,3,1),{f}_{z}(3,3,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-3,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,3,1)$, ${f}_{y}(3,3,1)$ and ${f}_{z}(3,3,1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,3,1) & =\\frac{(3)(1)}{2\\sqrt{(3)(3)(1)}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,3,1) & =\\frac{(3)(1)}{2\\sqrt{(3)(3)(1)}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{z}(3,3,1) & =\\frac{(3)(3)}{2\\sqrt{(3)(3)(1)}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈3+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-3,z-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.01,3.02,0.98)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01,y=3.02$ and $z=0.98,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,3.02,0.98) & ≈3+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,3.02-3,0.98-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈3+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & ≈3+(0.005+0.01-0.03) \\\\  & ≈2.985\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186335305 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1089 "q-129631" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{x}(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{y}(2,0)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0),$ estimate the value of $f(2.01,0.02).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1113 #uuid "9fa42cf0-7447-5a90-8bce-f362a8657065" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335304 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,0)+∇f(2,0)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,0),{f}_{y}(2,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.01,0.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.01$ and $y=0.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.01,0.02) & ≈⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.01-2,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =0(0.01)+2(0.02) \\\\  & =0+0.04 \\\\  & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335306 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335307 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335308 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335309 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335310 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1041 "selection" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186335306 13194139535171 true] [17592186335305 1110 #uuid "534a6971-06da-5c96-8f02-6ed553580def" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335306 1034 "0.04" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535171 true] [17592186335306 1111 #uuid "dd72d90b-0562-5162-8cb1-a9ff0e3b6ffb" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335307 1034 "0.08" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535171 true] [17592186335307 1111 #uuid "d753ba5