1 true] [17592186335332 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\pi ).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335332 1113 #uuid "bc87d41d-2d49-5be3-9ff6-3b6d8a36c9d1" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335332 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\pi )+∇f(-1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\pi )=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\pi )+∇f(-1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈1-(x+1)+0 \\\\  & ≈-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335334 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335335 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335336 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335337 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335338 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1041 "selection" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1104 #edb/ref 17592186335338 13194139535171 true] [17592186335333 1110 #uuid "f43888c0-7e64-5609-b7bb-7a66f0d2f8b0" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335334 1034 "f(x,y)≈2-\\pi +x+y" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535171 true] [17592186335334 1111 #uuid "f8e5