86335360 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,2)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,2)=-4,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,2)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02).$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335360 1113 #uuid "fe8cb7a5-a5d4-54a1-a22c-1aca559c19c7" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335360 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,2)+∇f(1,-1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1,2)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,2),{f}_{y}(1,-1,2),{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04,$ $y=-0.99,$ and $z=2.02$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,-0.99,2.02) & ≈6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,-0.99+1,2.02-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =6-0.16-0.01+0.06 \\\\  & =5.89\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335362 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335363 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335364 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335365 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186335366 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1041 "selection" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186335365 13194139535171 true] [17592186335361 1110 #uuid "a5d2338f-badc-5df1-9fe4-9a0ae2a68cec" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335362 1034 "6.12" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535171 true] [17592186335362 1111 #uuid "6b48622e-1287-57b6-b22b-5ff05131443f" 13194139535171 true] [17592186335363 1